en.fuller:
τέκνον, -ου, τό
(τίκτω), [in LXX chiefly for בֵּן, also for יֶלֶד, etc. ;]
that which is begotten, born (cf. Scottish bairn), a child of either sex: Mrk.13:12, Luk.1:7, Act.7:5; pl., Mat.7:11, Mrk.7:27, Luk.1:17, Eph.6:1, al.; τέκνα ἐπαγγελίας, Rom.9:8; τ. τῆς σαρκός, ib.; in a wider sense (as Heb. בָּנִים), of posterity, Mat.2:18, Luk.3:8, al.; specif., of a male child, Mat.21:28, Act.21:21, a,l.; in voc. as a form of kindly address from an elder to a junior or from a teacher to a disciple, Mat.9:2 21:28, Mrk.2:5, Luk.2:48; τ. μου (= cl. τ. μοι; see Bl., §37, 5), Gal.4:19 (τεκνία, WH, txt.), 2Ti.2:1. Metaphorical,
__(a) of disciples (apart from direct address, see supr.): Phm 10, 1Ti.1:2, Tit.1:4, 3Jo.4;
__(b) with reference to the Fatherhood of God (see: πατήρ, γεννάω), τέκνα τ. θεοῦ (cf. Isa.30:1, Wis.16:21): Rom.8:16, Eph.5:1, Php.2:15; and esp. in Johannine bks. (cf. Westc, Epp. Jo., 94, 120), Jhn.1:12, 1Jn.3:1 al.;
__(with) of those who imitate others and are therefore regarded as the spiritual offspring of their exemplars: Mat.3:9, Luk.3:8, Jhn.8:39, Rom.9:7, 1Pe.3:6; τ. διαβόλου, 1Jn.3:10;
__(d) as in Heb. (LXX, Jol.2:23, Psa.149:2, 1Ma.1:38), of the inhabitants of a city: Mat.23:37, Luk.13:34 19:44, Gal.4:25;
__(e) with an adjectival genitive, frequently rendering a Heb. expression, adopted from LXX or formed on the analogy of its language, but sometimes with parallels in Gk. writers (see Deiss., BS, 161ff.): τέκνα φωτός, Eph.5:8; τ. ὑπακοῆς, 1Pe.1:14; κατάρας, 2Pe.2:14; ὀργῆς, Eph.2:3.
SYN.: see: παῖς. (AS)

he.fuller:
τέκνον, -ου, τό
(τίκτω), [בשבעים בעיקר for בֵּן, also for יֶלֶד, ועוד ;]
that which is begotten, born (השוו Scottish bairn), a child of either sex: מרקוס.13:12, לוקס.1:7, מע״ש.7:5; pl., מתי.7:11, מרקוס.7:27, לוקס.1:17, אפ.6:1, ועוד; τέκνα ἐπαγγελίας, רומים.9:8; τ. τῆς σαρκός, שם; in a wider sense (as עב׳ בָּנִים), of posterity, מתי.2:18, לוקס.3:8, ועוד; specif., of a male child, מתי.21:28, מע״ש.21:21, a,l.; in voc. as a form of kindly address from an elder to a junior or from a teacher to a disciple, מתי.9:2 21:28, מרקוס.2:5, לוקס.2:48; τ. μου (= קלאסית τ. μοι; see Bl., §37, 5), גל.4:19 (τεκνία, WH, txt.), 2טי.2:1. Metaphorical,
(a) of disciples (apart from direct address, ר׳ לעיל): פימ  10, 1טי.1:2, טיט.1:4, 3יוח.4;
(b) with reference to the Fatherhood of God (ר׳: πατήρ, γεννάω), τέκνα τ. θεοῦ (השוו יש.30:1, חכמ.16:21): רומים.8:16, אפ.5:1, פי.2:15; and במיוחד in Johannine ספרים (השוו Westc, Epp. Jo., 94, 120), יוחנן.1:12, 1יוח.3:1 ועוד;
(with) of those who imitate others and are therefore regarded as the spiritual offspring of their exemplars: מתי.3:9, לוקס.3:8, יוחנן.8:39, רומים.9:7, 1פ.3:6; τ. διαβόλου, 1יוח.3:10;
(d) as in עב׳ (LXX, יואל.2:23, תה.149:2, 1מק.1:38), of the inhabitants of a city: מתי.23:37, לוקס.13:34 19:44, גל.4:25;
(e) with an adjectival יחס׳, לעיתים קרובות rendering a עב׳ expression, adopted from LXX or formed on the analogy of its language, but sometimes with parallels in Gk. writers (ר׳ Deiss., BS, 161ff.): τέκνα φωτός, אפ.5:8; τ. ὑπακοῆς, 1פ.1:14; κατάρας, 2פ.2:14; ὀργῆς, אפ.2:3.
נרדף: ר׳: παῖς. (AS)
